#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420352 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420350}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "89fae1b5-1405-51b4-b87d-75b6acbbdc2e" :question/math-academy-id "q-82192" :question/problem "Find $7det(A)-2det(B)$, where \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -0.5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- Since $A$ has two identical columns, we obtain\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,det(A)=0.\n$$\n\n- Since $B$ is a triangular matrix, its determinant is given by the product of the elements of the main diagonal: \n\n$$\ndet(B)=3\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 1\\cdot 2=-6\n$$\n\nTherefore, $7det(A)-2det(B)=7\\cdot 0-2\\cdot (-6)=12.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420353 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475337} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475338} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475339}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d2486be8-75b8-51f1-882e-08f8a79961f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-42463" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 6 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix given above. Identify **all** of the reasons why one can conclude that $|A|=0?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- $A$ does *not* have identical rows.\n\n- $A$ does *not* have proportional columns.\n\n- $A$ has a zero row: \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 6 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$A$ has a zero row” only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420713 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420712}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0b9ee9fe-348f-5bee-8c67-e9425237ec58" :question/math-academy-id "q-35759" :question/problem "Consider the set of matrices ${A,B,C},$ where \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -4 \\\\ 20 & -12\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ -7 & -5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \nIf the set is linearly dependent, find the value of $\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{2}}$ from a non-zero solution of ${x}_{1}A+{x}_{2}B+{x}_{3}C=O,$ where $O$ is the $2\\times 2$ zero-matrix." :question/worked-solution "If the set is linearly dependent, then there exist ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}$ not all zero such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}A+{x}_{2}B+{x}_{3}C=O.\n$$\n\nOtherwise, if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0,$ the set is linearly independent.\n\nSubstituting the matrices $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ into the above equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -4 \\\\ 20 & -12\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ -7 & -5\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} & 0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1} & -5{x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -4{x}_{2} \\\\ 20{x}_{2} & -12{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}4{x}_{3} & 5{x}_{3} \\\\ -7{x}_{3} & -5{x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}+4{x}_{3} & -4{x}_{2}+5{x}_{3} \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+20{x}_{2}-7{x}_{3} & -5{x}_{1}-12{x}_{2}-5{x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding entries, we obtain the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+4{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{2}+5{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+20{x}_{2}-7{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -5{x}_{1}-12{x}_{2}-5{x}_{3} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the corresponding augmented matrix and reduce it to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 20 & -7 & 0 \\\\ -5 & -12 & -5 & 0\\end{array}\\right] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+5{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 20 & -15 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -12 & 15 & 0\\end{array}\\right] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+5{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+(-3){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns are  the $1$st,  $2$nd and $3$rd. Therefore, we get the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+4{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{2}+5{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 10{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhich has only the zero solution ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n\nConsequently, ${A,B,C}$ is linearly independent."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420847 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420842}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0b58d794-d6a1-55fe-88d5-660b59bdd30a" :question/math-academy-id "q-42578" :question/problem "Find $det({A}^{-1}),$ if $A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "Given an invertible $n\\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet({A}^{-1})=\\frac{1}{det(A)}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $det(A):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =20-18 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\ndet({A}^{-1})=\\frac{1}{det(A)}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420848 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420841}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "37f105cd-9dd0-500b-bb29-7f9f80a1a14c" :question/math-academy-id "q-43071" :question/problem "Find $det({A}^{-1}),$ if $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "Given an invertible $n\\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet({A}^{-1})=\\frac{1}{det(A)}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $det(A):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =6-2 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\ndet({A}^{-1})=\\frac{1}{det(A)}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420849 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420840}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "38d108f6-377b-5951-bc6b-4ec43d8b652b" :question/math-academy-id "q-88281" :question/problem "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ find $det(A).$" :question/worked-solution "Given two $n\\times n$ matrices $M$ and $N,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet(MN)=det(M)\\cdot det(N).\n$$\n\nIn our case, this gives\n\n$$\ndet(A)=det\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot det\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that both matrices are triangular. Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =(3\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 2)\\cdot ((-1)\\cdot 3\\cdot (-4)) \\\\  & =(-6)\\cdot 12 \\\\  & =-72.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420850 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420846}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7f0a84be-936b-55f9-b169-54a768974c12" :question/math-academy-id "q-42577" :question/problem "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -7 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 6 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ find $det(A).$" :question/worked-solution "Given two $n\\times n$ matrices $M$ and $N,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet(MN)=det(M)\\cdot det(N).\n$$\n\nIn our case, this gives\n\n$$\ndet(A)=det\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot det\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -7 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 6 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe can compute the required determinants as follows:\n\n- For the first determinant, we can compute it using a Laplace expansion across the $3$rd row.\n\n- For the second determinant, we can compute it using a Laplace expansion down the $2$nd column.\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -7 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 6 & 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix}\\cdot 1(-1)^{2+2}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(2-1)\\cdot (8-6) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420851 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420845}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "80589b9c-bfb0-57bc-8e70-de307d877343" :question/math-academy-id "q-88306" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}300 & 250 & 100 \\\\ 300 & 270 & 100 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the $1$st row of our determinant is almost equal to the $2$nd row. So, we can rewrite the given determinant, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}300 & 250 & 100 \\\\ 300 & 270 & 100 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}300 & 250 & 100 \\\\ 300+0 & 250+20 & 100+0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, using the linearity property of determinants, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}300 & 250 & 100 \\\\ 300+0 & 250+20 & 100+0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}300 & 250 & 100 \\\\ 300 & 250 & 100 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+\\begin{vmatrix}300 & 250 & 100 \\\\ 0 & 20 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first determinant must be zero since it has two identical rows, while the second determinant can be expanded across the $2$nd row, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}300 & 250 & 100 \\\\ 300 & 250 & 100 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+\\begin{vmatrix}300 & 250 & 100 \\\\ 0 & 20 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & =0+20(-1)^{2+2}\\begin{vmatrix}300 & 100 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =20\\cdot (300\\cdot 1-100\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =20\\cdot 200 \\\\  & =4\\,000\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420852 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420843}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8c3dc4f1-ad56-595b-a7eb-41a85271c7fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-130693" :question/problem "Find\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}8 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 114 & 210 & 198 \\\\ 113 & 210 & 198\\end{vmatrix}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the $2$nd row of our determinant is almost equal to the $3$rd row. So, we can rewrite the given determinant, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 114 & 210 & 198 \\\\ 113 & 210 & 198\\end{vmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 113+1 & 210+0 & 198+0 \\\\ 113 & 210 & 198\\end{vmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, using the linearity property of determinants, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 113+1 & 210+0 & 198+0 \\\\ 113 & 210 & 198\\end{vmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 113 & 210 & 198 \\\\ 113 & 210 & 198\\end{vmatrix}+\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 113 & 210 & 198\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first determinant must be zero since it has two identical rows, while the second determinant can be expanded across the $2$nd row, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 113 & 210 & 198 \\\\ 113 & 210 & 198\\end{vmatrix}+\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 113 & 210 & 198\\end{vmatrix} & =0+1(-1)^{2+1}\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 210 & 198\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(4\\cdot 198-2\\cdot 210) \\\\  & =-(792-420) \\\\  & =-372\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420853 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420844}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9e9ddda3-97aa-551d-9453-cae76713ec59" :question/math-academy-id "q-43070" :question/problem "Find $det({A}^{-1}),$ if $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "Given an invertible $n\\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet({A}^{-1})=\\frac{1}{det(A)}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $det(A):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =10-5 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\ndet({A}^{-1})=\\frac{1}{det(A)}=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420854 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420839}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b1079762-fa86-56ef-a06d-de94245b17fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-130474" :question/problem "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle C=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 5 \\\\ -6 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ find $det(C).$" :question/worked-solution "Given two $n\\times n$ matrices $M$ and $N,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet(MN)=det(M)\\cdot det(N).\n$$\n\nIn our case, this gives\n\n$$\ndet(C)=det\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot det\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 5 \\\\ -6 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe can compute the required determinants as follows:\n\n- For the first determinant, we can compute it using a Laplace expansion across the $3$rd row.\n\n- For the second determinant, we can compute it using a Laplace expansion down the $2$nd column.\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(C) & =\\begin{vmatrix}6 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & 0 & 5 \\\\ -6 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}6 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\cdot (-1)(-1)^{2+2}\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(6-4)\\cdot (-1)(12-5) \\\\  & =4\\cdot (-7) \\\\  & =-28.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421528 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421527}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5b9c3557-0777-5517-a4eb-de326b8ca141" :question/math-academy-id "q-74609" :question/problem "Determine if the vector $u$ lies on the plane $Π:r=sb+tc$, $t,s\\in R$, where  $b=⟨-1,4,3⟩$, $c=⟨2,-6,-4⟩$, and  $u=⟨3,-7,-4⟩.$ In the affirmative case, find the value of $s\\cdot t$ from the decomposition $u=sb+tc.$" :question/worked-solution "By definition, $u\\in Π$ if there exist $t$ and $s$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sb+tc & =u \\\\ s\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -6 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -7 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-s+2t &=3 \\\\ 4s-6t &=-7 \\\\ 3s-4t &=-4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -6 & -7 \\\\ 3 & -4 & -4\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 3 & -4 & -4\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 5\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-s+2t &=3 \\\\ 2t &=5 \\\\ 0 &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n2t=5\\,⟹\\,t=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-s+2t & =3 \\\\ -s+2\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right) & =3 \\\\ -s+5 & =3 \\\\ -s & =-2 \\\\ s & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we found a solution $s=2,\\,t=\\frac{5}{2},$ we conclude that $u$ does indeed lie on the plane $Π.$ In fact, we have\n\n$$\nu=2b+\\frac{5}{2}c.\n$$\n\nFinally, $s\\cdot t=2\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}=5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421529 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421523}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9fca2b37-ca0d-5f9c-9cd0-c84b2660c1c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-34653" :question/problem "The vectors ${a}_{1}$, ${a}_{2}$, and $b$ are given below. Determine if $b\\in \\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}.$ In the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ from the decomposition $b={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}.$\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We know that $b\\in \\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ if there exist ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2} & =b \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2} &=10\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 10\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 10\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :=-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{2} &=-5.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-5.$ Substituting into the first equation gives \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+4(-5)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=20.\n$$\n\nSince the system has a solution, we can conclude that $b\\in \\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}.$\n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $b$ as a linear combination of ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20{a}_{1}+(-5){a}_{2} & =b \\\\ 20\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+(-5)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=20\\cdot (-5)=-100.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421530 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421525}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c75c18ab-94b6-560b-bd47-66c76f099710" :question/math-academy-id "q-105215" :question/problem "The vectors ${a}_{1}$, ${a}_{2}$, and $b$ are given below. Determine if $b\\in \\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}.$ In the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ from the decomposition $b={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}.$\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We know that $b\\in \\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ if there exist ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2} & =b \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2} &=3 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2} &=-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}3 & -6 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe second row of the augmented matrix now corresponds to the equation $0{x}_{1}+0{x}_{2}=1,$ which has no solution.\n\nSo, there are no values of ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2} & =b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $b$ does *not* lie in $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421531 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421526}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cdba03e5-45ad-5856-b84f-f9bccefe0c86" :question/math-academy-id "q-105242" :question/problem "Find the values of $k$ such that the vector $b$ lies in $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 18 \\\\ 39\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -6 \\\\ k\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We know that $b\\in \\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ if there exist ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2} & =b \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 18 \\\\ 39\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -6 \\\\ k\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+7{x}_{2} &=-3 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+18{x}_{2} &=-6 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}+39{x}_{2} &=k\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 7 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 18 & -6 \\\\ 5 & 39 & k\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 7 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 39 & k\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-5){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 7 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4 & k+15\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :=-\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 7 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & k+15\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-4){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 7 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & k+19\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third row of the augmented matrix now corresponds to the equation $0{x}_{1}+0{x}_{2}=k+19.$ Therefore, the system has a solution only if\n\n$$\n0=k+19\\,⇒\\,k=-19.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421532 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421524}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f6e087fb-be43-5d06-8b0f-af7c87178067" :question/math-academy-id "q-105241" :question/problem "Find the values of $k$ such that the vector $b$ lies in $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -3 \\\\ k\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We know that $b\\in \\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ if there exist ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2} & =b \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -3 \\\\ k\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-8{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2} &=5 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} &=-3 \\\\ -12{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2} &=k\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-8 & -3 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -3 \\\\ -12 & -6 & k\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}4 & 2 & -3 \\\\ -8 & -3 & 5 \\\\ -12 & -6 & k\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}4 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -12 & -6 & k\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}4 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & k-9\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third row of the augmented matrix now corresponds to the equation $0{x}_{1}+0{x}_{2}=k-9.$ Therefore, the system has a solution only if\n\n$$\n0=k-9\\,⇒\\,k=9.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421545 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421544}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e78f486a-816c-52d1-9895-59ae0c720ab7" :question/math-academy-id "q-130635" :question/problem "If $B=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$, then ${B}^{5}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute ${B}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{B}^{2} & =B\\cdot B \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -6 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that ${B}^{2}=B,$ so $B$ is an idempotent matrix. \n\nWe can use this fact to compute ${B}^{5}$ quickly, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{B}^{5} & ={B}^{2}\\cdot {B}^{2}\\cdot B \\\\  & =B\\cdot B\\cdot B \\\\  & ={B}^{2}\\cdot B \\\\  & =B\\cdot B \\\\  & ={B}^{2} \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{B}^{5}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423131 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423130}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "80e0e487-e8a8-5a08-9b5d-02833cb7fd41" :question/math-academy-id "q-105290" :question/problem "The set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is linearly dependent. Which of the following vectors could be ${a}_{2}$ if\n$$\n\\displaystyle {a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We say that two vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nA set containing *only two* vectors is linearly *dependent* if and only if the two vectors are parallel.\n\nFrom the given options, the correct answer is \n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo see why, notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}=3\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}=3{a}_{1}.\n$$\n\nThis way, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{a}_{1}-3{a}_{1} & =0 \\\\ 3{a}_{1}-{a}_{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are parallel.\n\nTo see why this means that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly dependent, we notice that the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nhas the solution ${x}_{1}=3$ and ${x}_{2}=-1,$ which is different from ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the set is linearly dependent."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423157 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423154}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2a383fb4-f81d-5536-bfab-907adbefb459" :question/math-academy-id "q-128372" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(7,4,-3)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n$$\n\\frac{x-3}{5}=\\frac{y-2}{4}=\\frac{z+1}{-2}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨7,4,-3⟩.$ From the canonical equation, we find that the line is parallel to the vector $⟨5,4,-2⟩.$ So, the normal vector of the plane is \n\n$$\nn=⟨5,4,-2⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨5,4,-2⟩ & =⟨7,4,-3⟩\\cdot ⟨5,4,-2⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨5,4,-2⟩ & =57\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨5,4,-2⟩ & =57 \\\\ 5x+4y-2z & =57 \\\\ 5x+4y-2z-57 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423177 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423176}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9509d4b2-8f43-5f25-8917-81a3d5d9b04d" :question/math-academy-id "q-33195" :question/problem "Find an equation of the plane that contains the point $P(0,1,1)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $\\mathbf{n}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the position vector of $P$ is $p=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $n=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ is normal to the plane.\n\nThe equation of the plane that passes though the point $P({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ and is perpendicular to the vector $n$ can be given as\n\n$$\n(r-p)\\cdot n=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the position vector of any point on the plane, and $p$ is the position vector of $P.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-p\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ r\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423620 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423618}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0c2bc939-4b4c-5161-9351-90818be5a0a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-75070" :question/problem "Given the matrix \n$V=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find a basis $B$ of $\\text{Col}(V).$" :question/worked-solution "In order to find a basis of $\\text{Col}(V),$ we reduce the matrix $V$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $2$nd columns are pivot columns, while the $3$rd column is a non-pivot column.\n\nRemember that we need to pick the pivot columns from the *original matrix*, not from the reduced one. \n\nThe $1$st and $2$nd columns from the original matrix are ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}.$ So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(V)$ is $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\},$ that is to say, \n\n$$\nB=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423621 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423619}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "693e6ed3-909b-52a6-8fa3-e0e6ae013327" :question/math-academy-id "q-75982" :question/problem "The matrix $F$ is given below. Let ${f}_{1}$, ${f}_{2}$,  ${f}_{3}$ and ${f}_{4}$ be the respective columns of $F.$\nDetermine if $B=\\left\\{{f}_{1},{f}_{2},{f}_{3}\\right\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(F).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}$, where ${f}_{4}={x}_{1}{f}_{1}+{x}_{2}{f}_{2}+{x}_{3}{f}_{3}.$ \n\n$$\nF=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -4 & -24 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -6 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $F$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -4 & -24 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -6 & -1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\left(-\\frac{1}{4}\\right){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 6 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -6 & -1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 6 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(F)$ is $\\{{f}_{1},{f}_{2}\\}.$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that $B=\\{{f}_{1},{f}_{2},{f}_{3}\\}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(F).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423670 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423669}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2d091317-3092-5633-8a6e-4652d2fd3389" :question/math-academy-id "q-106941" :question/problem "From top to bottom, fill in the blanks and hence complete the proof of the following statement using the axioms $[\\text{A}1]-[\\text{A}8]$ given below.\n\n*Statement:* \n\n*If $u$ and $v$ are vectors such that $x+u=x+v$ for some $x\\in V,$ then $u=v.$*\n\n*Proof:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+u & =x+v &  & [\\,\\text{given}\\,] \\\\ (x+u)+(-x) & =(x+v)+(-x) &  & [\\,\\text{add }-x\\text{ to both sides}\\,] \\\\ (u+x)+(-x) & =(v+x)+(-x) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,\\,] \\\\ u+(x+(-x)) & =v+(x+(-x)) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,\\,] \\\\ u+0 & =v+0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A4}\\,] \\\\ u & =v &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A3}\\,]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe axioms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ll}{\\color{black}\\text{[A1]}}:{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{y}=\\mathbf{y}+\\mathbf{x}} & {\\color{black}\\text{[A5]}}:{\\color{blue}1\\cdot \\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{x}} \\\\ {\\color{black}\\text{[A2]}}:{\\color{blue}(\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{y})+\\mathbf{z}=\\mathbf{x}+(\\mathbf{y}+\\mathbf{z})} & {\\color{black}\\text{[A6]}}:{\\color{blue}\\alpha (\\beta \\mathbf{x})=(\\alpha \\beta )\\mathbf{x}} \\\\ {\\color{black}\\text{[A3]}}:{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{0}=\\mathbf{x}} & {\\color{black}\\text{[A7]}}:{\\color{blue}(\\alpha +\\beta )\\mathbf{x}=\\alpha \\mathbf{x}+\\beta \\mathbf{x}} \\\\ {\\color{black}\\text{[A4]}}:{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{x}+(-\\mathbf{x})=\\mathbf{0}} & {\\color{black}\\text{[A8]}}:{\\color{blue}\\alpha (\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{y})=\\alpha \\mathbf{x}+\\alpha \\mathbf{y}}\\end{array}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the vector space axioms, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+u & =x+v &  & [\\,\\text{given}\\,] \\\\ (x+u)+(-x) & =(x+v)+(-x) &  & [\\,\\text{add }-x\\text{ to both sides}\\,] \\\\ (u+x)+(-x) & =(v+x)+(-x) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom }\\text{A1}\\,] \\\\ u+(x+(-x)) & =v+(x+(-x)) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom }\\text{A2}\\,] \\\\ u+0 & =v+0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A4}\\,] \\\\ u & =v &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A3}\\,]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423671 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423668}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "87c89c45-3edb-5cd5-88f1-a3ee2b4d8003" :question/math-academy-id "q-35640" :question/problem "Let $T$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ matrices $({a}_{ij})$  with real entries, such that  ${a}_{11}+{a}_{22}$ is rational.  Complete the following sentence:\n\n*$T$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to addition of matrices and multiplication of matrices by a real number) because $\\text{____}\\,.$*" :question/worked-solution "The correct answer is \n\n*$T$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to addition of matrices and multiplication of matrices by a real number) because **multiplication by a scalar is not closed.*** \n\n For example, the identity matrix \n\n$$\nI=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nlies in $T$, but\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{2}\\cdot I=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\notin T\n$$\n\nbecause $\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{2}$ is not a rational number.\n\nOn the other hand,\n\n- The zero matrix belongs to $T.$  $\\,✓$\n\n- Addition of matrices with real entries is commutative, in general.    $\\,✓$\n\n- Addition of matrices with real entries is associative, in general.    $\\,✓$\n\n- Given any $A\\in T,$  the additive inverse matrix $-A=(-1)\\cdot A$ is also in  $T,$ and   $A+(-A)=O.$  $\\,✓$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423960 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423959}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e005b4ab-002a-57d1-b9b7-3fc85eeb395f" :question/math-academy-id "q-70819" :question/problem "Given the Cartesian equation $x-2y+3z=0$ of the plane $\\mathrm{Π}$, find an equation of $\\mathrm{Π}$ in the form $(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p})\\cdot \\mathbf{n}=0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, from the equation of the plane we get that the normal vector of $Π$ is $n=⟨1,-2,3⟩.$\n\nNow, we solve the Cartesian equation of $Π$ for $z$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2y+3z & =0 \\\\ 3z & =-x+2y \\\\ z & =-\\frac{1}{3}x+\\frac{2}{3}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we put, say, $x=0$ and $y=0$, we get $z=0.$ Hence, the point $P(0,0,0)$ lies on $Π.$ Note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨0,0,0⟩.$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ (r-⟨0,0,0⟩)\\cdot ⟨1,-2,3⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424110 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424109}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b249af0d-2baa-56c1-b03f-659223bf8b34" :question/math-academy-id "q-35572" :question/problem "The matrix $A$ and the vector $b$ are given below.\nDetermine if $b\\in \\text{Col}(A).$ \nIn the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}$, where ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers such that $b={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3},$ and ${a}_{1}$, ${a}_{2}$, and ${a}_{3}$ are the respective columns of $A.$\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & -8 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(A),$ we need to check if the equation $Ax=b$ is consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[A\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}[A\\,|\\,b] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 3 & -8 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -4 & 2\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 3 & -8 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 12 & 6\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-6){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 3 & -8 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -12 & -6\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}-8{x}_{3} &=-2 \\\\ -{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3} &=2 \\\\ -12{x}_{3} &=-6.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Substituting it into the second equation gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{2}+4\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) & =2 \\\\ -{x}_{2}+2 & =2 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, from the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3(0)-8\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) & =-2 \\\\ {x}_{1}-4 & =-2 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we have a solution, the equation $Ax=b$ is consistent, which implies that $b\\in \\text{Col}(A).$ \n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $b$ as a linear combination of ${a}_{1},$ ${a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{b}=2\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{{a}_{1}}+0\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{{a}_{2}}+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}}_{{a}_{3}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}=2\\cdot 0\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424594 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424592}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4780154a-e6c5-5212-9585-41f4b2c7d38a" :question/math-academy-id "q-34832" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{dim}\\left(\\text{Span}\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}\\right)=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}5 & -10 & 15 \\\\ -1 & 5 & -3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -10 & 15 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $2$ pivot columns (or alternatively, it has exactly $2$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore, \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{dim}\\left(\\text{Span}\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}\\right)=2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424595 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424593}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f427e4e2-13a5-5d7b-8b8d-103e6b378823" :question/math-academy-id "q-79320" :question/problem "Determine the dimension of $$\n\\displaystyle \\text{Span}\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, our matrix is already in a row echelon form.\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $3$ pivot columns (or alternatively, $3$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore, the dimension of the span is $3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424899 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424898}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "200dace6-387b-586f-87ab-a5501c01cbe0" :question/math-academy-id "q-130851" :question/problem "Given that $det(A)=det(B),$ which of the following elementary row operations transforms matrix $A$ to matrix $B,$ where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since $det(A)=det(B)$, the row operation applied to $A$ must be to add or subtract a multiple of one row to another row. In other words, it is of the form \n\n$$\n{R}_{j}:={R}_{j}+(k){R}_{i},\\,i\\ne j.\n$$\n\nBy considering the given row operations that are of the above form, we notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 5\\end{vmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =det(B).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\n{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425055 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425050}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "06e82198-39dc-5c74-b637-e63de9108225" :question/math-academy-id "q-34763" :question/problem "If $D=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,$ then $\\text{rank}(D)=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the rank of $D,$ we first reduce it to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix has $3$ pivot columns (or alternatively, it has exactly $3$ non-zero rows).\n\nTherefore, $\\text{rank}(D)=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425056 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425051}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6826463c-847d-5512-b760-3fbeb596f635" :question/math-academy-id "q-34805" :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $7\\times 12$ matrix that has $6$ **pivot** columns. What is the rank of the matrix?" :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\text{rank}(A)$ is the number of pivot columns in $A.$ \n\nSince we are told that $A$ has $6$ pivot columns, we have \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)=6.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425057 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425052}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6e4ed9e5-eccc-52bc-8945-b31db9b251e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-35285" :question/problem "If \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -6 & -9 & -4 \\\\ 4 & 6 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$, then $\\text{rank}(A)=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the rank of $A,$ we first reduce it to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -6 & -9 & -4 \\\\ 4 & 6 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 6 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix has $2$ pivot columns (or alternatively, it has exactly $2$ non-zero rows).\n\nTherefore, $\\text{rank}(A)=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425058 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425049}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8c046845-0154-54e8-9266-c14fc31a0fbc" :question/math-academy-id "q-79560" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices has a rank of $3?$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that a matrix of rank $3$ must have at least $3$ columns and at least $3$ rows. \nSo, we disregard the following matrices:\n\n- $A$ since it has only $2$ rows.\n\n- $B$ since it has only $2$ rows (and only $2$ columns).\n\n- $E$ since it has only $2$ columns.\n\nAlso, the matrix must have $3$ pivot columns. As we can see, the matrix $C$ has exactly $3$ pivot columns, while the matrix $D$ has only $2$ pivot columns. So, $\\text{rank}(C)=3$ and $\\text{rank}(D)=2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the matrix $C.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425059 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425047}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a35aea07-c94b-5a19-9d28-82b8ec3131d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-139966" :question/problem "If \n$W=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 & -4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -4 & 6 \\\\ -1 & -6 & 4 & -7\\end{bmatrix}$, then $\\text{rank}(W)=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the rank of $H,$ we first reduce it to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 & -4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -4 & 6 \\\\ -1 & -6 & 4 & -7\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 6 & -4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix has $3$ pivot columns (or alternatively, it has exactly $3$ non-zero rows).\n\nTherefore, $\\text{rank}(W)=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425060 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425054}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cf208166-634c-5dd4-a095-ee064f03b9e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-79559" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices has a rank of $5?$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that a matrix of rank $5$ must have at least $5$ columns and at least $5$ rows. \nSo, we disregard the following matrices:\n\n- $B$ since it has only $2$ rows.\n\n- $C$ since it has only $3$ columns.\n\n- $D$ since it has only $3$ rows (and only $3$ columns).\n\nAlso, the matrix must have $5$ pivot columns. As we can see, the matrix $A$ has exactly $5$ pivot columns, while the matrix $E$ has only $4$ pivot columns. So, $\\text{rank}(A)=5$ and $\\text{rank}(E)=4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the matrix $A.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425061 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425048}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d99c604a-a10b-57d3-8148-d6702b5326c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-79157" :question/problem "Let $B$ be a $4\\times 5$ matrix that has $3$ **pivot** columns. What is the rank of the matrix?" :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\text{rank}(B)$ is the number of pivot columns in $B.$ \n\nSince we are told that $B$ has $3$ pivot columns, we have \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(B)=3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425062 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425053}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e56cf627-f5bb-57b0-ae99-521a4ce7bf5a" :question/math-academy-id "q-79405" :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $8\\times 9$ matrix that has $4$ **pivot** columns. What is the rank of the matrix?" :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\text{rank}(A)$ is the number of pivot columns in $A.$ \n\nSince we are told that $A$ has $4$ pivot columns, we have \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)=4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425154 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425153}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ea940e2f-7cf1-5b69-9241-9cde37e5f341" :question/math-academy-id "q-140503" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,Q=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & \\sqrt{2} & -2 \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & -1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,R=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${p}_{12}\\ne {p}_{21},$ we obtain $P\\ne {P}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- $Q$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $Q={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $R$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $R={R}^{T}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$Q$ and $R$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425352 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425351}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cd765ff8-911d-50bf-a599-8cf7e9df87e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-77807" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}-4 & 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 6 & 5 & -3 \\\\ 8 & -9 & 6 & 7\\end{vmatrix}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using a cofactor expansion along the $4$th column, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}-4 & 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 6 & 5 & -3 \\\\ 8 & -9 & 6 & 7\\end{vmatrix} & =0\\cdot {C}_{14}+0\\cdot {C}_{24}+(-3)\\cdot {C}_{34}+7\\cdot {C}_{44} \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot (-1)^{3+4}\\begin{vmatrix}-4 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 8 & -9 & 6\\end{vmatrix}+7\\cdot (-1)^{4+4}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-4 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 4 & 6 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-4 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 8 & -9 & 6\\end{vmatrix}+7\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-4 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 4 & 6 & 5\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425353 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425350}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f2f19b4d-d8c8-5ecf-9fc4-030f5c14f82f" :question/math-academy-id "q-77803" :question/problem "Compute the following determinant using a cofactor expansion along the $3$rd row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using cofactor expansion along the $3$rd row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & =0\\cdot {C}_{31}+0\\cdot {C}_{32}+4\\cdot {C}_{33}+3\\cdot {C}_{34} \\\\  & =4\\cdot (-1)^{3+3}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+3\\cdot (-1)^{3+4}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the first $3\\times 3$ determinant by applying a Laplace expansion along the $2$nd row, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & =0\\cdot {C}_{21}+(-2)\\cdot {C}_{22}+(-4)\\cdot {C}_{23} \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot (-1)^{2+2}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+(-4)\\cdot (-1)^{2+3}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+4\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2(6-0)+4(0-2) \\\\  & =-12-8 \\\\  & =-20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the second  $3\\times 3$ determinant by applying a Laplace expansion along the $3$rd row, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} & =1\\cdot {C}_{31}+0\\cdot {C}_{32}+-1\\cdot {C}_{33} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{3+1}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{vmatrix}+(-1)\\cdot (-1)^{3+3}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{vmatrix}-\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-6+2)-(-6-0) \\\\  & =-4+6 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}=4(-20)-3(2)=-86.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425404 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425400}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "83850766-9b1b-5a37-bf38-12d47d12c4d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-35347" :question/problem "Given $$\n\\displaystyle G=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 & 4 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 3 & -6 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 5 & 1 \\\\ -6 & -15 & -12 & -16\\end{bmatrix},\\,\n$$ what is $\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(G))?$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(G))$, we reduce $G$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}G & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 & 4 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 3 & -6 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 5 & 1 \\\\ -6 & -15 & -12 & -16\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 8 & -2 & 7 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 5 & 1 \\\\ -6 & -15 & -12 & -16\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{4} & :={R}_{4}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 8 & -2 & 7 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -7\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 8 & -2 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{9}{2} & \\frac{11}{4} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -7\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix has $4$ pivot columns and no non-pivot columns. \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(G))=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425405 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425403}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "85da25d6-54a2-55b2-b4ae-23095c708e42" :question/math-academy-id "q-79338" :question/problem "If $B$ is a $4\\times 6$ matrix that has $4$ **non-pivot** columns, then $\\text{dim(Null}(B))=$" :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\text{dim(Null}(B))$ is the number of non-pivot columns in $B.$\n\nSince we are told that $B$ has $4$ non-pivot columns, we have \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(B))=4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425406 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425401}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b000b980-8b9a-5a4e-a59b-6de7017a3b28" :question/math-academy-id "q-34901" :question/problem "If $A$ is a $7\\times 11$ matrix that has $7$ **non-pivot** columns, then $\\text{dim(Null}(A))=$" :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\text{dim(Null}(A))$ is the number of non-pivot columns in $A.$\n\nSince we are told that $A$ has $7$ non-pivot columns, we have \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(A))=7.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425407 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425402}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fd86946f-a577-5d18-8f57-7b7435272376" :question/math-academy-id "q-35200" :question/problem "Consider the matrices given below. Find $\\,\\text{dim(Null}(A))+\\text{dim(Null}(B))+\\text{dim(Null}(C)).$\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 8 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 7 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The dimension of the null space of a matrix is the number of non-pivot columns in the matrix. \n\nAlso, all of the given matrices are in row echelon form.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the statements.\n\n- The matrix $A$ has $2$ pivot columns and no non-pivot column. So, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(A))=0.\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ has $2$ pivot columns and $1$ non-pivot column (namely, the $3$rd column). So, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(B))=1.\n$$\n\n- The matrix $C$ has $1$ pivot column and $2$ non-pivot columns (namely, the $2$nd and $3$rd columns). So, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(C))=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(A))+\\text{dim(Null}(B))+\\text{dim(Null}(C))=0+1+2=3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425728 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475134} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475135} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475136} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475137}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3ea10c69-304c-5ad4-bd04-4affc2cf325b" :question/math-academy-id "q-157376" :question/problem "Let $L$ be the line in ${R}^{3}$ that passes through the points $(0,0,0)$ and $(-1,3,1).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nFirst, note that the direction vector of the line $L$ is \n\n$$\nd=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis means that the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-t \\\\ 3t \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in R,$ and the corresponding Cartesian equation is \n\n$$\n-x=\\frac{y}{3}=z.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement “$L$ contains the zero-vector” is true. Indeed, from the definition of $L$ we have that the point $(0,0,0)$ is in $L.$\n\n- Statement “$L$ is closed under vector addition” is true. Let, for example, $a=\\begin{bmatrix}-a \\\\ 3a \\\\ a\\end{bmatrix}\\in L$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}-b \\\\ 3b \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}\\in L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =\\begin{bmatrix}-a \\\\ 3a \\\\ a\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-b \\\\ 3b \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}(-a)+(-b) \\\\ 3a+3b \\\\ a+b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-(a+b) \\\\ 3(a+b) \\\\ a+b\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the conditions $-x=\\frac{y}{3}=z.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in L.$\n\n- Statement “$L$ is closed under scalar multiplication” is true. Let, for example, $a=\\begin{bmatrix}-a \\\\ 3a \\\\ a\\end{bmatrix}\\in L$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-a \\\\ 3a \\\\ a\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}k(-a) \\\\ k(3a) \\\\ ka\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-(ka) \\\\ 3(ka) \\\\ ka\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the conditions $-x=\\frac{y}{3}=z.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in L.$\n\n- Statement “$L$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}$” is true. Since statements “$L$ is closed under vector addition” and “$L$ is closed under scalar multiplication” are true, $L$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"All the statements are true.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425877 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425874}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "eec69fbf-a0cd-50e6-9b17-0f0c96351008" :question/math-academy-id "q-69953" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 5 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The $1$st row of the matrix contains three zeros, more than any other row. So using a Laplace expansion along the $1$st row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 5 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & =0\\cdot {C}_{11}+0\\cdot {C}_{12}+1\\cdot {C}_{13}+0\\cdot {C}_{14} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{1+3}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -1 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -1 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425949 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425948}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "db005b4e-86cd-5194-b2fd-10ca4ee59c03" :question/math-academy-id "q-42021" :question/problem "Determine the value of $k$ for which the following system of linear equations is consistent dependent:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2y &=3 \\\\ -3x-(k+1)y &=-9\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -(k+1) & -9\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -(k+1) & -9\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -k+5 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1-2y &=3 \\\\ -(k-5)y &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe system is consistent dependent if it has infinitely many solutions. For the system to have infinitely many solutions, it must have at least one free variable.\n\nIf $k=5,$ then the second equation is $0=0,$ which is true for any value of $y$, and we can remove this equation from the system. As a result, the system will contain only one equation, $x+2y=3.$ \n\nThen, the basic variable will be $x,$ and we will have a free variable $y.$ That means the system will have infinitely many solutions.\n\nTherefore, our system is consistent dependent when $k=5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426072 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475107} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475108} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475109}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "251cfe7e-474d-59b2-8ccf-101401daf068" :question/math-academy-id "q-139978" :question/problem "Consider the following sentence:\n\n*If $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and $\\det(A)=0,$ then $\\underline{\\phantom{xxxxxx}}\\,.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⇔\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\text{Col}(A) &={R}^{5} \\\\ \\text{dim(Col}(A)) &=5=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\ \\text{Null}(A) &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) &=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the options separately.\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and $\\det(A)=0,$ then $A$ has no inverse.* $\\,✓$ \n\nIndeed, since $\\det(A)=0,$ the matrix $A$ can't be invertible.\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and $\\det(A)=0,$ then $\\text{Null}(A)$ has dimension zero.* $\\,\\times$\n\nSince $A$ is not invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\text{Null}(A)\\ne \\{0\\}$ which, in turn,  means that $\\text{Null}(A)$ must have a basis so the dimension must be greater than zero.\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and $\\det(A)=0,$ then $A$ has less than $5$ pivot columns.* $\\,✓$\n\nIndeed, since $A$ is not invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\text{dim(Col}(A))\\ne 5,$ which means that $A$ has less than $5$ pivot columns.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$A$ has no inverse”; “$A$ has less than $5$ pivot columns” only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426073 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426069}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "33a20d40-e8ca-5cc3-bf01-9f700d36b547" :question/math-academy-id "q-141191" :question/problem "$$\nA\\text{ is invertible}\\,⇔\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\text{Col}(A) &={R}^{n} \\\\ \\text{dim(Col}(A)) &=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\ \\text{Null}(A) &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) &=\\bbox[3pt,white,border: 2px solid Gainsboro]{\\phantom{W}}=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the equivalence above, where $A$ is an $n\\,\\times \\,n$ matrix. Which of the following should be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the *invertible matrix theorem* states the following: \n\n*Let $A$ be an $n\\,\\times \\,n$ matrix. Then we have the following equivalence:*\n\n$$\nA\\text{ is invertible}\\,⇔\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\text{Col}(A) &={R}^{n} \\\\ \\text{dim(Col}(A)) &=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\ \\text{Null}(A) &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) &=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426074 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426068}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "71223fcf-7212-5023-82ea-b55cb18fa0b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-142468" :question/problem "$$\nA\\text{ is invertible}\\,⇔\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\text{Col}(A) &={R}^{n} \\\\ \\text{dim(Col}(A)) &=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\ \\text{Null}(A) &=\\bbox[3pt,white,border: 2px solid Gainsboro]{\\phantom{W}} \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) &=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the equivalence above, where $A$ is an $n\\,\\times \\,n$ matrix. Which of the following should be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the *invertible matrix theorem* states the following: \n\n*Let $A$ be an $n\\,\\times \\,n$ matrix. Then we have the following equivalence:*\n\n$$\nA\\text{ is invertible}\\,⇔\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\text{Col}(A) &={R}^{n} \\\\ \\text{dim(Col}(A)) &=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\ \\text{Null}(A) &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) &=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $\\{0\\}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426075 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426067}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "937a6bb1-f955-5ee0-bbd6-d97686bceb6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-142471" :question/problem "$$\nA\\text{ is invertible}\\,⇔\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\text{Col}(A) &={R}^{n} \\\\ \\text{dim(Col}(A)) &=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\ \\text{Null}(A) &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) &=\\bbox[3pt,white,border: 2px solid Gainsboro]{\\phantom{W}}=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the equivalence above, where $A$ is an $n\\,\\times \\,n$ matrix. Which of the following should be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the *invertible matrix theorem* states the following: \n\n*Let $A$ be an $n\\,\\times \\,n$ matrix. Then we have the following equivalence:*\n\n$$\nA\\text{ is invertible}\\,⇔\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\text{Col}(A) &={R}^{n} \\\\ \\text{dim(Col}(A)) &=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\ \\text{Null}(A) &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) &=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426076 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426071}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9fbccd10-1c43-51a4-ad8b-36063e11427d" :question/math-academy-id "q-142367" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ is a solution of the equation $Ax=0?$\n\nI. $\\text{dim(Null}(A))\\ne 0$\n\nII. $A$  is  invertible\n\nIII. $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{4}$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⇔\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\text{Col}(A) &={R}^{4} \\\\ \\text{dim(Col}(A)) &=4=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\ \\text{Null}(A) &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) &=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn. \n\n- Statement I is true. Since $v\\ne 0$ and $Av=0,$ \nthen $v\\in \\text{Null}(A)$ and, therefore, $\\text{dim(Null}(A))\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\text{dim(Null}(A))\\ne 0,$ then according to the invertible matrix theorem, the matrix $A$ can't be invertible.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $A$ is not invertible, then by the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\text{Col}(A)\\ne {R}^{4}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426077 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186454440} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186454441} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186454442}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a0f098cb-e297-54fe-a435-e8a16d43aa06" :question/math-academy-id "q-139977" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -{x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}\\right.\\,|\\,{x}_{1}\\in R\\}?\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⇔\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\text{Col}(A) &={R}^{4} \\\\ \\text{dim(Col}(A)) &=4=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\ \\text{Null}(A) &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) &=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $\\text{dim(Null}(A))=1$ is true. Indeed, since \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)=\\{{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\,|\\,{x}_{1}\\in R\\}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(A))=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement “$A$ is invertible” is false. Since $\\text{dim(Null}(A))\\ne 0,$ then according to the invertible matrix theorem, the matrix $A$ can't be invertible.\n\n- Statement $\\text{rank}(A)<4$ is true. Indeed, since $A$ is not invertible, then by the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\text{rank}(A)\\ne 4,$ or equivalently $\\text{rank}(A)<4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\text{dim(Null}(A))=1$; $\\text{rank}(A)<4$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426308 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426307}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "60094f6c-d2bd-58df-8627-15176f255263" :question/math-academy-id "q-104435" :question/problem "The entry ${a}_{21}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & k & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nequals $2.$ Find the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & k & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -1\\end{vmatrix} & =1\\begin{vmatrix}k & 2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{vmatrix}-0\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}+1\\begin{vmatrix}2 & k \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(-k-6)-0+1(6-k) \\\\  & =-k-6+6-k \\\\  & =-2k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{21}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{12}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{12} & =(-1)^{1+2}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(-2-2) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{21}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-2k}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that ${a}_{21}^{′}=2$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{k} & =2 \\\\ -2 & =2k \\\\ -\\frac{2}{2} & =k \\\\ k & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426309 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426306}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ccf4f432-0d42-54bc-b36a-bd39fd754033" :question/math-academy-id "q-73478" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\ngiven that $det(A)=-3.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's follow the standard five-step procedure.\n\n**Step 1:** We are given the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A)=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Form the matrix of the minors of $A.$\nTo make this matrix, we replace each entry of $A$ by its minor.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 8 & -3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Create the matrix of cofactors by changing the sign of some of the entries of $M$ according to the rule of alternating signs.\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -8 & -3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 4:** Write down the transpose of the matrix of cofactors.\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -8 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 5:** Use the formula for the inverse of a $3\\times 3$ matrix.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -8 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426531 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475138} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475139} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475140}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "24da091b-82da-56f5-873d-6d9cc1e779e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-158072" :question/problem "Which of the diagrams shown below represent subspaces of ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$ or ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I does not represent a subspace. The origin $0=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ clearly lies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram II does not represent a subspace. The origin $0=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ clearly lies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram III represents a line that passes through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is diagram III only"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426532 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475053} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475054} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475055} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186669294}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "61609b84-fa99-5574-b117-b0c4a018b4cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-110456" :question/problem "Which of the diagrams shown below represent subspaces of ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I does not represent a subspace. The vector $a=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ lies inside the given subset. But \n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot a=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nlies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram II represents a set that does not contain the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so Diagram II is not a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram III represents the whole plane ${R}^{2}$ without the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so Diagram III is not a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"None.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426533 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475062} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475063} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475064}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "784a3447-3ec5-5b3c-aa05-c4909c41863a" :question/math-academy-id "q-110763" :question/problem "Which of the following sets is a subspace of ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each of the subsets.\n\n- The set $A$ is not a subspace. It represents a line, and a line is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. If the origin belongs to this subset, we would have\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1+t \\\\ t-1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhich gives us\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}t+1 &=0 \\\\ t-1 &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe see that the system has no solution. So, $0$ is not in $A.$\n\n- The set $B$ is not a subspace. Indeed, both $a=⟨1,0⟩$ and $b=⟨0,1⟩$ belong to $B$ but \n\n$$\na+b=⟨1,0⟩+⟨0,1⟩=⟨1,1⟩\n$$\n\n \ndoes not satisfy the condition $xy=0$ since $1\\cdot 1\\ne 0.$\n\n- The set $C$ is a subspace. It represents a line (the $y$-axis), and a line is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. Here, the components of the zero vector $0=⟨0,0⟩$ satisfy the equation $x=0$ since $0=0.$ So, $0$ lies in $C.$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is $C=\\{⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:x=0\\}$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426534 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475141} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475142} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475143}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8d538e48-6d40-5139-9e4a-4a2045c240b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-158073" :question/problem "Which of the diagrams shown below represent subspaces of ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I represents a plane that passes through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- Diagram II represents a line that does not pass through the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so the line in Diagram II is not a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- Diagram III represents a line that passes through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is diagrams I and III only"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426535 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475059} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475060} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475061}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c109cc3b-b729-5d92-be15-5107dfc714ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-110759" :question/problem "Which of the following sets is a subspace of ${R}^{3}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each of the subsets.\n\n- The set $A$ is a subspace. It represents a plane, and a plane is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. Here, the components of the zero vector $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ satisfy the equation $x-y+z=0$ since $0-0+0=0.$ So $0$ lies in $A.$\n\n- The set $B$ is a subspace. This subset has only one element, the zero vector $0=⟨0,0,0⟩,$ which is a special (trivial) subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The set $C$ is not a subspace. This subset has only one element, the vector $⟨0,0,1⟩,$ so it does not contain the zero vector.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{gathered}A=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:\\\\x-y+z=0\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered};\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{gathered}B=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:\\\\{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=0\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}\n$$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426536 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475056} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475057} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475058}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d4fcfc53-7279-50f5-90f1-bd8c9662d8f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-110599" :question/problem "Which of the diagrams shown below represent subspaces of ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$ or ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I does not represent a subspace. The origin $0=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$  clearly lies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram II does not represent a subspace. There are vectors  $a=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$  in the subset for which ${a}_{1}>0$ and ${a}_{2}>0.$   But then\n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot a=\\begin{bmatrix}-{a}_{1} \\\\ -{a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nlies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram III is a line that passes through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is diagram III only"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426856 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426854}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "973ba0d1-71e0-5bd1-b707-8f3f23076709" :question/math-academy-id "q-34761" :question/problem "Find the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{b}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix},\\,{b}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}$ then what is the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find ${x}_{1}$,  ${x}_{2}$, and ${x}_{3}$ such that  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+{x}_{3}{b}_{3} & =x \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom this, we get the system with the augmented matrix $M$, which we reduce using Gaussian elimination: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & \\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 4 & 0 & 6 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 0 & -6\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\ ∼ & \\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 4 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 12 & 2 & 16 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 0 & -6\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\ ∼ & \\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 4 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 12 & 2 & 16 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2} &=6 \\\\ 12{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=16 \\\\ {x}_{3} &=2.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=2.$ Substituting this into the second  equation yields\n\n$$\n12{x}_{2}+2(2)=16\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=1.\n$$\n\n Finally,  by substituting ${x}_{2}=1$ into the first equation we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+4(1)=6\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=2+1+2=5.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426857 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426855}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ad22bfb4-da83-594b-af3c-ca1a05478310" :question/math-academy-id "q-112247" :question/problem "Find the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,{b}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ then what is the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2} & =x \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above gives the system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 9\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 9\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 15\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2} &=2 \\\\ -5{x}_{2} &=15.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-3.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-2(-3)=2\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=(-4)+(-3)=-7.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427385 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427384}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2ffb7e93-f33c-5adf-acf0-f55e6fbbb059" :question/math-academy-id "q-36831" :question/problem "Determine all real eigenvalues $\\lambda $ of the matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle B=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the matrix $\\,B-\\lambda I:$\n\n\n$$\nB-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}-1-\\lambda  & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is $\\det(B-\\lambda I)=0.$ \n\nSince the determinant of a triangular (or diagonal) matrix is given by the product of the entries on the main diagonal, we obtain the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det(B-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-1-\\lambda  & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )(4-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)(\\lambda -4)(\\lambda -1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1,$ $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=4,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=1.$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvalues of a triangular (or diagonal) matrix are always the entries on the main diagonal."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427559 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427557}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0010b93b-f332-5a69-9522-ddec46829e95" :question/math-academy-id "q-110852" :question/problem "Consider the vectors ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},$ ${a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},$ and ${a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-16 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find a basis $B$ of $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find a subset of linearly independent vectors. So, we start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 8 & -16 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, our matrix is already in a row echelon form.\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $2$nd columns are pivot columns, while the $3$rd column is a non-pivot column.\n\nThe $1$st and $2$nd columns from the original matrix are ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}.$ So, a basis of $S$ is $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\},$ that is to say, \n\n$$\nB=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427560 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427558}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d7ffe635-df11-5df3-b5e3-505ac6562519" :question/math-academy-id "q-110895" :question/problem "The vectors ${a}_{1},$ ${a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are given below. Determine whether $B=\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}.$ If that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ where ${a}_{3}={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}.$\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 17\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We want to determine if $B$ is indeed a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}.$ So, we want to find ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in R$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}}_{{a}_{1}}+{x}_{2}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}}_{{a}_{2}}=\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 17\\end{bmatrix}}_{{a}_{3}}.\n$$\n\nThis equation is equivalent to the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} &=5 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2} &=17\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the augmented matrix of the system to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 7 & 17\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -8 & -8\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :=-\\frac{1}{8}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the RREF matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So,  $B$ is indeed a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}.$\nAlso, the matrix tells us that ${x}_{1}=2$ and ${x}_{2}=1,$ meaning that\n\n$$\n2\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}}_{{a}_{1}}+1\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}}_{{a}_{2}}=\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 17\\end{bmatrix}}_{{a}_{3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=2\\cdot 1=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427561 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427556}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dd91b565-0528-53ef-9896-e748c3fd0d1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-75068" :question/problem "Consider the vectors ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},$ ${a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 16\\end{bmatrix},$ and ${a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find a basis $B$ of $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find a subset of linearly independent vectors. So, we start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 10 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 16 & 6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 16 & 6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $2$nd columns are pivot columns, while the $3$rd column is a non-pivot column.\n\nRemember that we need to pick the pivot columns from the *original matrix*, not from the reduced one.\n\nThe $1$st and $2$nd columns from the original matrix are ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}.$ So, a basis of $S$ is $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\},$ that is to say, \n\n$$\nB=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 16\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427636 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427635}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "df55035e-2530-507b-9e8e-793ed8f0bafc" :question/math-academy-id "q-82284" :question/problem "Find $-4det(A)+3det(B)$, where \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & \\sqrt{3} & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{2} & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- Since $A$ has a zero column, we obtain\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & \\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,det(A)=0.\n$$\n\n- Since $B$ is a triangular matrix, its determinant is given by the product of the elements of the main diagonal: \n\n$$\ndet(B)=\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot 3=6\n$$\n\nTherefore, $-4det(A)+3det(B)=-4\\cdot 0+3\\cdot 6=18.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429769 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429768}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "293eb96a-a168-5847-97d0-8739c8726172" :question/math-academy-id "q-74977" :question/problem "Consider the vectors ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},$ and ${a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find a basis of $S=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ by inspection." :question/worked-solution "Remember that a basis of $S$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that spans $S.$\n\nFrom the given vectors, we can see that ${a}_{1}$ is a linear combination of vectors ${a}_{2}$ and ${a}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=1\\cdot {a}_{2}+1\\cdot {a}_{3}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the vector ${a}_{1}$ can be removed from $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ without changing the span. So $S=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is spanned by just $\\{{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}.$ \n\nLastly, note that ${a}_{2}$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent since they are not parallel. So the set $\\{{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is a linearly independent set that spans $S.$\n\nTherefore, a basis of $S$ is $\\{{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB=\\left\\{\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}}_{{a}_{2}},\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{a}_{3}}\\right\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429817 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429816}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3c3b23dc-4bcb-57b2-b0fc-dd67734d3e36" :question/math-academy-id "q-128493" :question/problem "Find a normal vector to the plane\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 17 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+s\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}t,\\hspace{0.167em}s\\in (-\\mathrm{∞},\\mathrm{∞}).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "From the parametric equation, we have that $v=⟨2,-7,1⟩$ and $w=⟨3,5,0⟩$ are parallel to the plane.\n\nNow, we need to find a normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of $v$ and $w$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =v\\times w \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\times \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -7 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-5i+3j+31k \\\\  & =⟨-5,3,31⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨-5,3,31⟩.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429818 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429815}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8d62e579-1b65-5746-996a-3e6e877f180c" :question/math-academy-id "q-158107" :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the $4$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the vectors\n\n$$\na=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,c=\\begin{bmatrix}13 \\\\ 11 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,d=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's construct the matrix $M,$ whose columns are the coordinates of our vectors $a,$ $b,$ $c,$ and  $d:$\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 5 & 13 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 11 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these vectors equals the absolute value of the determinant of $M:$\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. So, we have\n\n$$\ndet(M)=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 5 & 13 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 11 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=(-7)\\cdot (-2)\\cdot (-5)\\cdot 1=-70.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|=|-70|=70.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429941 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429940}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e59ecbbf-3ac2-51fb-8da8-83763d787a9a" :question/math-academy-id "q-71033" :question/problem "Find a parametric equation of the plane that passes through the points $D(1,0,-2),\\hspace{0.167em}E(-2,-2,5)$, and $F(-2,4,3).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find two vectors that are parallel to the plane (but not to each other). Let's use the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{DE}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{DF},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{DE} & =⟨-2,-2,5⟩-⟨1,0,-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3,-2,7⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{DF} & =⟨-2,4,3⟩-⟨1,0,-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3,4,5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if we consider the position vector $d=⟨1,0,-2⟩$ of the point $D,$ then the corresponding parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =d+s\\overset{\\to }{DE}+t\\overset{\\to }{DF} \\\\ r & =⟨1,0,-2⟩+s⟨-3,-2,7⟩+t⟨-3,4,5⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430000 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429997}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1df900d6-620c-58af-b345-1b8940d52367" :question/math-academy-id "q-110981" :question/problem "Consider the matrix given by $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Determine the value of $k$ for which the vector $b=\\begin{bmatrix}-20 \\\\ -14 \\\\ k\\end{bmatrix}$ belongs to $\\text{Col}(A).$" :question/worked-solution "If $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(A),$ then the equation $Ax=b$ must be consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[A\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}[A\\,|\\,b] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 3 & -20 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -14 \\\\ -1 & 1 & k\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 3 & -20 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -14 \\\\ 0 & 4 & k-20\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 3 & -20 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -14 \\\\ 0 & 0 & k+8\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} &=-20 \\\\ 2{x}_{2} &=-14 \\\\ 0 &=k+8.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nIn order for this system to be consistent, we require $0=k+8,$ which means that $k=-8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430001 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429998}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6e653566-d375-5ee1-b745-3dfd829381b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-111042" :question/problem "The matrix $C$ and the vector $b$ are given below.\nDetermine if $b\\in \\text{Col}(C).$ \nIn the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}$, where ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers such that $b={x}_{1}{c}_{1}+{x}_{2}{c}_{2}+{x}_{3}{c}_{3},$ and ${c}_{1}$, ${c}_{2}$, and ${c}_{3}$ are the respective columns of $C.$\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(C),$ we need to check if the equation $Cx=b$ is consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[C\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}[C\\,|\\,b] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}4 & 1 & -4 & 9 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 & -8 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 & 5\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & ↔{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 2 & 3 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 & -8 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -4 & 9\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 2 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -4 & 9\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 2 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -7 & -16 & -11\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 2 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -9 & -18\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=5 \\\\ 3{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=-3 \\\\ -9{x}_{3} &=-18.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=2.$ Substituting it into the second equation gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{2}+3(2) & =-3 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, from the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =5-2{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3} \\\\  & =5-2(-3)-3(2) \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we have a solution, the equation $Cx=b$ is consistent, which implies that $b\\in \\text{Col}(C).$ \n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $b$ as a linear combination of ${c}_{1},$ ${c}_{2},$ and ${c}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}}_{b}=5\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{c}_{1}}+(-3)\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{{c}_{2}}+2\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{{c}_{3}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}=5\\cdot (-3)\\cdot 2=-30.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430002 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429999}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fa3eae70-d536-57c6-818d-d88d2dc98925" :question/math-academy-id "q-110965" :question/problem "The matrix $K$ and the vector $b$ are given below. Determine if $b\\in \\text{Col}(K).$ In the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ where ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are real numbers such that $b={x}_{1}{k}_{1}+{x}_{2}{k}_{2},$ and ${k}_{1},$ ${k}_{2}$ are the respective columns of $K.$\n\n$$\nK=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(K),$ we need to check if the equation $Kx=b$ is consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[K\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}[K\\,|\\,b] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} &=2 \\\\ 2{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 0 &=3.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get the false statement $0=3.$  \n\nTherefore, the system is inconsistent, which implies that $b\\notin \\text{Col}(K).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430731 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430730}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7fd80750-80bf-5e1d-b08d-abec51a32d9d" :question/math-academy-id "q-112213" :question/problem "Find the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,{b}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 20\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ then what is the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2} & =x \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 20\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above gives the system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 2 & -5 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 20\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 2 & -5 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 20\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 2 & -5 \\\\ 0 & -10 & 40\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} &=-5 \\\\ -10{x}_{2} &=40.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-4.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+2(-4)=-5\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=3+(-4)=-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430732 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430729}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8a5b5cd1-19ba-524a-960c-18e8a17d885d" :question/math-academy-id "q-112202" :question/problem "Find the vector $x$, if $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\},$ and \n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{b}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We know that any vector in  $\\text{Span}\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ has the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the coordinates relative to the basis $\\,B.$ Since we are told that $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\underbrace{(\\,3\\,)}_{{x}_{1}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\underbrace{(\\,-3\\,)}_{{x}_{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -24 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -12 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -36 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430827 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186454750} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186454751} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186454752}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "48a8881c-e6ac-54a2-9375-24bf5efbb3a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-79325" :question/problem "Let ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ be linearly independent vectors, and suppose that the vector $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that a basis of a vector space $V$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that span $V.$ The dimension of $V$ is the number of vectors in any basis of $V$ (if such a basis exists). \n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the statements.\n\n- Statement “${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ form a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}$” is true. Indeed, $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}$ is spanned by ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}.$ Moreover, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ are linearly independent vectors. So,  ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ is a basis of the span.\n\n- Statement $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\})=2$ is false. Indeed, $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is spanned by ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$, and we are told that ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ are linearly independent vectors. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ is a basis of the span. Therefore, we have $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\})=3$ (not $2$).\n\n- Statement $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},w\\})=3$ is true. We are told that $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}.$ This means that $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},w\\}$ is completely spanned by the vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ only. So, $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},w\\})=3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ form a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}$”; $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},w\\})=3$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430828 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430825}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6f3e3a92-ac43-52ed-a6de-e1847c3b16d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-35300" :question/problem "Determine the dimension of\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{Span}\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 6 & -9 \\\\ -4 & 6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $1$ pivot column (or alternatively, it has exactly $1$ non-zero row). \n\nTherefore, the dimension of the span is $1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430829 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430824}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fd38fa95-000b-54ff-9226-2d221d8be579" :question/math-academy-id "q-79321" :question/problem "Determine the dimension of $$\n\\displaystyle \\text{Span}\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, our matrix is already in a row echelon form.\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $1$ pivot column (or alternatively, $1$ non-zero row). \n\nTherefore, the dimension of the span is $1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430969 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430968}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d6084cfc-eb77-5ae0-9ec3-a6e87e13ee7f" :question/math-academy-id "q-34980" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -7\\end{bmatrix}$ and the vector $b=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ k\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find the value of $k$ such that $b\\in \\text{Null}(A).$" :question/worked-solution "If $b\\in \\text{Null}(A),$ then we must have $Ab=0.$ Substituting the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ab & =0 \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -7\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ k\\end{bmatrix} & =0 \\\\ -7k & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for this equation, we obtain $k=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433149 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433148}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "93aa26c6-5456-5313-9c30-05baafe99b46" :question/math-academy-id "q-81112" :question/problem "Consider the orthogonal set $U$ below. By adding a new vector $x=[{x}_{1},\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}]^{T},$ extend the set so that it forms an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{3}.$ What is the value of $\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{2}}?$\n\n$$\nU=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $U$ as ${u}_{1},\\,{u}_{2}.$ To extend the given set to an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{3}$, we need to find a third vector $x=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}$ such that\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot x &=0 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot x &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}+-2{x}_{2} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system above and reduce it to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}4 & 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\left(-\\frac{1}{4}\\right){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{5}{2} & -\\frac{3}{4} & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-\\frac{3}{10}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}+2(-\\frac{3}{10}{x}_{3})+3{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\left(-\\frac{3}{5}\\right){x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{5}{x}_{3} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{10}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=10,$ we obtain the vector \n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{3}$ is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{2}}=\\frac{-6}{-3}=2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433150 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475334} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475335} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475336} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186669395}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9cc85c8f-2fc1-51b5-90b3-b4c40aaa9ac5" :question/math-academy-id "q-40288" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are orthogonal?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of the given sets as \n$\\{{u}_{1},{u}_{2},{u}_{3}\\},$ \n$\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\},$ and \n$\\{{w}_{1},{w}_{2},{w}_{3}\\},$ respectively. \n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthogonal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0,\\,{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{3}=0,\\,{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{3}=0.\n$$\n\n- ${v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2}=0.$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=0,\\,{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{3}=0,\\,{w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{3}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The set $U$ cannot be orthogonal since it contains  $3$ vectors from ${R}^{2}$ (in which we can have an orthogonal set with at most 2 non-zero vectors). Therefore, $U$ is not an orthogonal set.\n\n- For the set $V,$ notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=0+10=10\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $V$ is not an orthogonal set.\n\n- For the set $W,$ notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=12+3-1=14\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $W$ is not an orthogonal set.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"None of the listed\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433151 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433145}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9e056f00-625c-54f9-921d-feb61293e496" :question/math-academy-id "q-83768" :question/problem "Consider the **orthonormal** set $U$ below. By adding a new vector $x=[{x}_{1},\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}]^{T},$ extend the set so that it forms an **orthonormal** basis of ${R}^{3}.$ What is the value of $|{x}_{1}|+|{x}_{2}|+|{x}_{3}|?$\n\n$$\nU=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{21}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{21}} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{21}}\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $U$ as ${u}_{1},\\,{u}_{2}.$ To extend the given set to an orthonormal basis of ${R}^{3}$, we need to find a third vector $x$ such that $‖x‖=1,$ and\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot x &=0 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot x &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}{x}_{2}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{21}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{21}}{x}_{2}-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{21}}{x}_{3} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system above and reduce it to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{21}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{21}} & -\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{21}} & 0\\end{array}\\right] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=\\sqrt{6}\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=\\sqrt{21}\\cdot {R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -4 & 0\\end{array}\\right] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -6 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-2{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+2(-2{x}_{3})+{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=3{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}_{3} \\\\ -2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=1,$ we obtain the vector \n$$\n\\displaystyle x=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ \n\nNow, we need to normalize $x.$ Notice that $‖x‖=\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\sqrt{14}.$ So, we normalize $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{‖x‖}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, an orthonormal basis of ${R}^{3}$ is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{21}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{21}} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{21}}\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $$\n\\displaystyle |{x}_{1}|+|{x}_{2}|+|{x}_{3}|=|\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{14}}|+|-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}}|+|\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}|=\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{14}}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433152 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475110} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475111} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475112}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9f013658-0e98-56e3-b0df-e2d26e152fca" :question/math-academy-id "q-140479" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are orthogonal?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of the given sets as \n$\\{{u}_{1},{u}_{2}\\},$ \n$\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\},$ and \n$\\{{w}_{1},{w}_{2}\\},$ respectively. \n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthogonal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0.$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2}=0,\\,{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{3}=0,\\,{v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{3}=0.\n$$\n\n- ${w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=-3+0-2+2=-3\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $U$ is not an orthogonal set.\n\n- For the set $V,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}=4-4=0 & ✓ \\\\ {v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{3} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=2+0-4+2=0 & ✓ \\\\ {v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{3} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=0+6-4-2=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $V$ is orthogonal.\n\n- For the set $W,$ notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=2+2+4=8\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $W$ is not an orthogonal set.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"$V$ only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433153 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475385} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475386} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475387}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bccef2e2-efdc-5751-b184-d287795e3e5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-82399" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are orthonormal?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of the given sets as \n$\\{{u}_{1},{u}_{2}\\},$ \n$\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\},$ and \n$\\{{w}_{1},{w}_{2}\\},$ respectively. \n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthonormal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1} &=1 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2} &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2} &=0 \\\\ {v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{3} &=0 \\\\ {v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,\\text{and}\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1} &=1 \\\\ {v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2} &=1 \\\\ {v}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{3} &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{1} &=1 \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{2} &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}=0 & ✓ \\\\ {u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=1 & ✓ \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=1 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $U$ is orthonormal.\n\n- For the set $V,$ notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}=0+12+6=18\\ne 1 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $V$ is not an orthonormal set.\n\n- For the set $W,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{5}\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{12}{25}-\\frac{12}{25}=0 & ✓ \\\\ {w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{16}{25}+\\frac{9}{25}=1 & ✓ \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{5}\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{9}{25}+\\frac{16}{25}=1 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $W$ is an orthonormal set.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"$U$ and $W$ only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433173 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475328} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475329} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475330} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186669387}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "46f6694f-8103-554b-a6d3-9a98e8c2973d" :question/math-academy-id "q-34711" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nAssume that ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -5{x}_{1}+5{x}_{3} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}2 & 1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 5 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}-5 & 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -3 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{2}{5}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}-5 & 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}-5 & 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we conclude that the basic variables are ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$, while ${x}_{3}$ is the free variable. \n\nSince there is one free variable, the equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has an infinite number of nonzero solutions, which means that the vectors are linearly dependent. \n\nTherefore, statement “The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent” is false, while statements “The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly dependent” and “The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$  has an infinite number of nonzero solutions” are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433491 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433489} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433490}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ee4392fe-5c34-5631-8fcd-93d0be346505" :question/math-academy-id "q-181693" :question/problem "![](images/3f/3f9807dae43519dbd9407130c51cb1473a36c46a42d8c433d9b4d88edb4e382d.png)\n\nUse set builder notation to describe the shaded region given in the diagram above:\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,x\\,{{field-2}}\\,2{y}^{2}\\}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The points in our set include all those that lie *to the right* of the curve $x=2{y}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, a point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following condition is satisfied:\n\n$$\nx>2{y}^{2}\n$$\n\nSince the boundary is not included in the set (the curve is dashed), the inequality is strict.\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in R,y\\in R,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\times R={R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,x>2{y}^{2}\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434595 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475331} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475332} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475333}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8f1ed82d-67ec-55b7-826d-30733bc2c88f" :question/math-academy-id "q-35575" :question/problem "Which of the following sets is a subspace of ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each of the subsets.\n\n- The set $A$ is a subspace. It represents a line, and a line is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. Here, the components of the zero vector $0=⟨0,0⟩$ satisfy the equation $y=0$ since $0=0.$ So, $0$ lies in $A.$\n\n- The set $B$ is a subspace. It represents a line, and a line is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. Here, the components of the zero vector $0=⟨0,0⟩$ satisfy the equation $y=3x$ since $0=3\\cdot 0.$ So, $0$ lies in $B.$\n\n- The set $C$ is not a subspace because it does not contain the zero vector. Indeed, $0=⟨0,0⟩$ does not satisfy the condition $y=2$ since $0\\ne 2.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is $A=\\{⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:y=0\\}$; $B=\\{⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:y=3x\\}$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435080 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435077}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4527164d-e7cb-52d0-bbe2-36d132296780" :question/math-academy-id "q-132004" :question/problem "The inverse of the matrix $N$ is found using row operations, as shown below. What are the row operations corresponding to the blank spaces?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}[N\\,|\\,I] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}-2 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 1 & 0\\end{array}\\right] &  & \\underline{\\phantom{xxxxxx}} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 & 2\\end{array}\\right] &  & \\underline{\\phantom{xxxxxx}} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write down $N$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $N$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}[N\\,|\\,I] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}-2 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 1 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 & 2\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding row operations are\n\n$$\n{R}_{1}↔\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${N}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{N}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435147 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435146}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0807a9e2-ba3b-5d6e-8562-a580bc61f62a" :question/math-academy-id "q-104515" :question/problem "Solve the following system of linear equations by finding the inverse of the coefficient matrix:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+y-2z &=-1 \\\\ 3x-z &=5+3y \\\\ 3z-2y &=6-x\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFind  $x+y+z.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite the system in the correct order:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+y-2z &=-1 \\\\ 3x-3y-z &=5 \\\\ x-2y+3z &=6\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, we  write the system in the form $Ax=b$, where $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $x$ and $b$ are $3\\times 1$ column vectors. We can do this as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$ So, we compute ${A}^{-1}$ as follows:\n\n*Step 1:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}-1\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}+(-2)\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(-9-2)-(9+1)-2(-6+3) \\\\  & =-22-10+6 \\\\  & =-26.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 2:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}3 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{vmatrix}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-11 & 10 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 8 & -5 \\\\ -7 & 4 & -9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 3:*\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}-11 & -10 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 8 & 5 \\\\ -7 & -4 & -9\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Step 4:*\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}-11 & 1 & -7 \\\\ -10 & 8 & -4 \\\\ -3 & 5 & -9\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Step 5:*\n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T}=-\\frac{1}{26}\\begin{bmatrix}-11 & 1 & -7 \\\\ -10 & 8 & -4 \\\\ -3 & 5 & -9\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to the given system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{26}\\begin{bmatrix}-11 & 1 & -7 \\\\ -10 & 8 & -4 \\\\ -3 & 5 & -9\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{26}\\begin{bmatrix}-26 \\\\ 26 \\\\ -26\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x+y+z=1-1+1=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435148 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435144}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0c58ffa5-e4d4-5bf5-8b35-8a71f48534ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-78565" :question/problem "Consider a matrix equation $B\\,x=A\\,d,$ where $A$ and $B$ are $2\\times 2$ non-singular matrices, while $x$ and $d$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors. Solve the equation for $x.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $B$ is not singular, it must have an inverse ${B}^{-1}.$ Now, pre-multiplying both sides of the equation $B\\,x=A\\,d$ by ${B}^{-1},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B\\,x & =A\\,d \\\\ {B}^{-1}B\\,x & ={B}^{-1}A\\,d \\\\ {I}_{2}\\,x & ={B}^{-1}A\\,d \\\\ x & ={B}^{-1}A\\,d.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435149 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435145}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e321af2f-d67a-5dc4-a077-c6060845ea99" :question/math-academy-id "q-78553" :question/problem "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x+2y+z &=3 \\\\ x-2y+z &=1 \\\\ 2x-y+2z &=5\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe system above has the matrix representation $Ax=b.$ Given that $x=⟨x,y,z⟩$ is unique, what is the value of $x+y+z?$ \n\n*Hint: The inverse of the coefficient matrix is given by*\n\n$$\n\\,{A}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 7 & -8\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's write our system in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax & =b \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 7 & -8\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution is $x=-1,y=1,z=4.$\n\nFinally, $x+y+z=-1+1+4=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435356 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435355}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "60c7ad14-43d6-5b2c-81c2-60bb8c562302" :question/math-academy-id "q-75086" :question/problem "The matrix $Q$ is given below. Let ${q}_{1}$, ${q}_{2}$, and ${q}_{3}$ be the respective columns of $Q.$ Determine if $B=\\left\\{{q}_{1},{q}_{2}\\right\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(Q).$ If that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$, where ${q}_{3}={x}_{1}{q}_{1}+{x}_{2}{q}_{2}.$\n\n$$\nQ=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 8 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $Q$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 8 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 8 & 24\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{8}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(Q)$ is $\\{{q}_{1},{q}_{2}\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the $3$rd column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express ${q}_{3}$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{{q}_{3}}=\\underbrace{(2)}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}}_{{q}_{1}}+\\underbrace{(3)}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{{q}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=2\\cdot 3=6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435357 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435354}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8e2ef874-d6ff-53a7-81b2-d096ed751911" :question/math-academy-id "q-75090" :question/problem "The matrix $Q$ is given below. Let ${q}_{1}$, ${q}_{2}$,  ${q}_{3}$, and ${q}_{4}$ be the respective columns of $Q.$ Determine if $B=\\left\\{{q}_{1},{q}_{2}\\right\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(Q).$ If that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$, where ${q}_{4}={x}_{1}{q}_{1}+{x}_{2}{q}_{2}.$\n\n$$\nQ=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $Q$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & -\\frac{5}{2} & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -\\frac{5}{8} & \\frac{3}{4} \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(Q)$ is $\\{{q}_{1},{q}_{2}\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the $4$th column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express ${q}_{4}$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{{q}_{4}}=\\underbrace{\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{{q}_{1}}+\\underbrace{(1)}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{{q}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us $$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot 1=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435358 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435353}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fa6c549b-f344-52f9-9015-63f22befbecb" :question/math-academy-id "q-111501" :question/problem "The matrix $W$ is given below. Let ${w}_{1}$, ${w}_{2}$, and ${w}_{3}$ be the respective columns of $W.$\nDetermine if $B=\\left\\{{w}_{1},{w}_{3}\\right\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(W).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}$, where ${w}_{2}={x}_{1}{w}_{1}+{x}_{3}{w}_{3}.$\n\n$$\nW=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -9 & 6 \\\\ -5 & 15 & 10\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $W$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -9 & 6 \\\\ -5 & 15 & 10\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 15 & 10\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+5{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 20\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{20}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $3$rd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(W)$ is $\\{{w}_{1},{w}_{3}\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the $2$nd column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express ${w}_{2}$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ 15\\end{bmatrix}}_{{w}_{2}}=\\underbrace{(-3)}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}}_{{w}_{1}}+\\underbrace{(0)}_{{x}_{3}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}}_{{w}_{3}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}=(-3)\\cdot 0=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435676 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186454756} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186454757} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186454758}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ea4581af-a083-51da-803f-0f83b3ed2ea6" :question/math-academy-id "q-79349" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and  $\\det(A)=1?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⇔\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\text{Col}(A) &={R}^{5} \\\\ \\text{dim(Col}(A)) &=5=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\ \\text{Null}(A) &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) &=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn. \n\n- Statement “$A$ is not invertible” is false. Indeed,   a matrix $A$ is invertible if and only if $\\det(A)\\ne 0.$ Since here we have $\\det(A)=1\\ne 0,$ our matrix $A$ must be  invertible.\n\n- Statement $\\text{rank}(A)=4$ is false. Indeed, by the invertible matrix theorem\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\text{rank}(A)=5.$\n\n- Statement “$A$ has $5$ pivot columns” is true. Since $\\text{rank}(A)=5$,  it must be $\\text{dim(Col}(A))=5$ which, in turn, means that  $A$ has $5$ pivot columns.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$A$ has $5$ pivot columns” only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435767 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435763}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3797960f-4f46-583b-afe6-1bb724ee5f4b" :question/math-academy-id "q-130641" :question/problem "Find\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 4 & -7 \\\\ 144 & 158 & 103 \\\\ 144 & 158 & 102\\end{vmatrix}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the $2$nd row of our determinant is almost equal to the $3$rd row. So, we can rewrite the given determinant, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 4 & -7 \\\\ 144 & 158 & 103 \\\\ 144 & 158 & 102\\end{vmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 4 & -7 \\\\ 144+0 & 158+0 & 102+1 \\\\ 144 & 158 & 102\\end{vmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, using the linearity property of determinants, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 4 & -7 \\\\ 144+0 & 158+0 & 102+1 \\\\ 144 & 158 & 102\\end{vmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 4 & -7 \\\\ 144 & 158 & 102 \\\\ 144 & 158 & 102\\end{vmatrix}+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 4 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 144 & 158 & 102\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first determinant must be zero since it has two identical rows, while the second determinant can be expanded across the $2$nd row, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 4 & -7 \\\\ 144 & 158 & 102 \\\\ 144 & 158 & 102\\end{vmatrix}+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 4 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 144 & 158 & 102\\end{vmatrix} & =0+1(-1)^{2+3}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 144 & 158\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(2\\cdot 158-4\\cdot 144) \\\\  & =-(316-576) \\\\  & =260\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435768 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435766}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "788f9347-638e-5e07-bc97-727179291d72" :question/math-academy-id "q-130780" :question/problem "Given that \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}a & b & c \\\\ -3 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}=3,\n$$\nfind \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}a-2 & b & c \\\\ -3 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We \"factor out\" the given determinant from the determinant we would like to find:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}a-2 & b & c \\\\ -3 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}a-2 & b+0 & c+0 \\\\ -3 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the linearity property of determinants and expanding the second determinant across the $1$st row, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}a-2 & b+0 & c+0 \\\\ -3 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & =\\underbrace{\\begin{vmatrix}a & b & c \\\\ -3 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}}_{3}+\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3-2(-1)^{1+1}\\begin{vmatrix}5 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3-2\\cdot (5+1) \\\\  & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435769 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435764}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8501cdd8-7879-5e81-a6ba-962c1d7f9129" :question/math-academy-id "q-130532" :question/problem "Find $|{A}^{-1}|,$ if $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & -3 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "Given an invertible $n\\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have\n\n$$\n|{A}^{-1}|=\\frac{1}{|A|}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $|A|$ using a Laplace expansion across the $2$nd row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A| & =\\begin{vmatrix}-5 & -3 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2(-1)^{2+1}\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 6\\end{vmatrix}+1(-1)^{2+2}\\begin{vmatrix}-5 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{vmatrix}+2(-1)^{2+3}\\begin{vmatrix}-5 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(-18+16)+(-30-4)-2(20+3) \\\\  & =-4-34-46 \\\\  & =-84\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n|{A}^{-1}|=\\frac{1}{|A|}=-\\frac{1}{84}.\n$$"}]}
